Description: The Wild OS installer window: a glass sheet, steps without a page, progress for QML
 Three changes to the Calamares shell that the Wild OS installer design
 needs.
 .
 Sheet mode. With windowExpanding: fullscreen and a productWallpaper in
 branding.desc, the window covers the screen with the wallpaper, dimmed and
 scaled to cover, and the installer is a rounded sheet of windowSize in the
 middle, with a blurred glass backdrop and a shadow drawn by the window
 itself. Panels and QML pages get transparent backgrounds so the glass shows
 through: QML pages on the sheet are QQuickWidgets instead of window
 containers, which cannot be transparent. The stylesheet pads the page area
 instead of per-step margins. The confirm and cancel questions become
 frameless sheets with the title as their first line. A QML panel may set
 its own width, as it already could its height, and the navigation panel
 may be up to 96 pixels high.
 .
 Pass-through steps. A view module with passThrough: true in its
 configuration has no page: next() and back() run its onActivate() and
 onLeave() and move past it, so one page can show the answers of several
 modules while each keeps its jobs. The list lives in global storage
 (passThroughSteps), and ViewManager.setPassThrough() lets a page skip
 itself when it has nothing to ask.
 .
 QML pages. The JobQueue is registered as a singleton in io.calamares.core,
 so a slideshow can show the progress and the job name itself, and
 GlobalStorage.insert() can be called from QML, so a page can store its
 answers for the jobs. ExecutionViewStep stores each install module's share
 of the overall progress (execModuleWeights), so the install page can show
 which stage is running without showing job names.
Author: Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>
Forwarded: no

